# Mobile env template.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Use `.env`, not `.env.local`. Expo reads these files in order, highest
# priority first:
#   .env.<mode>.local  →  .env.local  →  .env.<mode>  →  .env
# <mode> is NODE_ENV: `development` for `expo start`, `production` for builds
# and exports. Expo skips `.env.local` in `test` mode. This repo commits no
# `.env` file, so a second file has no purpose. Use one file.
#
# Only `EXPO_PUBLIC_*` variables reach the client bundle. Metro replaces each
# name by literal text match at build time. It replaces
# `process.env.EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL`, but not `process.env[name]`, so
# write every name in full. The bundle ships to every device. Never put a
# secret behind the prefix.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS FILE CONFIGURES THE LOCAL METRO DEV SERVER ONLY.
#
# System environment variables override dotenv files, so EAS builds ignore
# this file. Git also ignores it, so it never uploads.
#
# Set each REQUIRED key (Supabase) in `eas.json` as well, under the `preview`,
# `production` and `development` profiles. A build without them points at
# nothing.
#
# `development-simulator` does not need them. It builds locally and runs in a
# simulator on the same machine, where Metro is always available.
#
# `development` does need them. It is an internal build that can run on a
# device with no dev server. The app then reads the manifest from build time,
# and EAS never had a `.env`, so `extra` is empty and lib/env.ts throws at
# launch. When Metro is connected it serves its own manifest built from THIS
# file, and the app ignores the baked values.
#
# You can omit an optional key (PostHog) from `eas.json` until you provision
# it, but never set it to "". `eas build` rejects an empty value with "not
# allowed to be empty". Omit the key instead: lib/env.ts reads an absent key
# as unset. `eas-config.test.ts` enforces both rules.
#
# A new `EXPO_PUBLIC_*` key is a two-place edit: this file and `eas.json`.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Supabase. The default is the remote project.
#
# REMOTE (default). Point at the hosted dev project and leave it there. No
# containers to run, and no context to change between the app and the
# dashboard. Read both values from the project API settings:
#   https://supabase.com/dashboard/project/_/settings/api
#
# LOCAL. Use this only for a throwaway database, such as a test of a
# destructive migration, or offline work. Run `pnpm db:start`, then
# `pnpm db:status` to read the API URL and publishable key. The local URL is:
#   http://127.0.0.1:54321
# `pnpm db:reset` deletes all local data. Migrations stay in
# supabase/migrations for both options. Write schema changes there, never in
# the dashboard.
#
# Supabase deprecates the legacy `anon` and `service_role` JWTs at the end of
# 2026. Use the publishable key (`sb_publishable_...`) here. It is safe in a
# client bundle and does not bypass RLS. The `sb_secret_...` key does bypass
# RLS. Never put it behind an `EXPO_PUBLIC_` prefix, or anywhere in this app.
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# PostHog. Leave blank to disable analytics. Without a key, lib/analytics.ts
# builds no client and every capture() call does nothing, so local development
# needs no key.
#
# The project API key is write-only and safe in the bundle. The personal API
# key (`phx_...`) is not. Never put it here.
EXPO_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_API_KEY=
# Defaults to https://eu.i.posthog.com when unset. We use PostHog EU Cloud to
# keep event data in the EU, which our GDPR compliance requires. Do not use
# us.i.posthog.com. The project key is region-specific, so a US host rejects
# our events without an error.
#
# Set this only to override for a self-hosted instance.
EXPO_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST=
